  All checks pass. Here's the summary:

  New page: rotating_zonohedra.html

  Source: the Wolfram notebook Zonohedron.nb, whose "Table" section renders zonohedra via Zonohedron[NonparallelVectors[PolyhedronVertices[#]]] for every Platonic and Archimedean solid. I reproduced that algorithm in JavaScript.

  Algorithm (generic, runs in-browser):
  1. Generators — dedupe the source solid's vertex directions, treating v and −v as the same direction (the NonparallelVectors step).
  2. Vertices — sample directions on a Fibonacci sphere, and for each compute the sign pattern σₖ = sign(d·gₖ); each distinct pattern gives one vertex Σ σₖ gₖ. This enumerates every region of the great-circle arrangement → all zonohedron vertices.
  3. Faces — one opposite pair per generator pair (i,j) with normal gᵢ×gⱼ; face vertices are recovered directly from the sign-pattern map (free indices = generators perpendicular to the face normal), so it's fast and handles degenerate (merged) faces correctly.
  4. Render — painter's-algorithm depth sort, back-face cull, diffuse lighting, optional edges — same aesthetic as the rhombic-icosahedron page.

  16 zonohedra in a dropdown, generated from: Tetrahedron, Cube, Octahedron, Dodecahedron, Icosahedron, Truncated Tetrahedron, Cuboctahedron, Truncated Cube, Truncated Octahedron, Rhombicuboctahedron, Truncated Cuboctahedron, Snub Cube, Icosidodecahedron, Truncated Icosahedron, Truncated Dodecahedron,
  Truncated Icosidodecahedron.

  Verified outputs match known zonohedra:
  • Icosahedron → rhombic triacontahedron (32 V, 30 F) ✓
  • Cube → rhombic dodecahedron (14 V, 12 F) ✓
  • Octahedron → parallelepiped (8 V, 6 F) ✓

  Template chrome from rotating_ellipse8.html: full-viewport canvas, top-left controls panel, gtag snippet, contextmenu guard, requestAnimationFrame loop with a degrees/sec speed slider (clamped dt).

  Controls: solid selector (shows live V/F/generator counts), rotation speed, scale, tilt, light angle, show-edges toggle, reset.

  Two solids omitted — the rhombicosidodecahedron and snub dodecahedron. Their vertex coordinates require the plastic constant (cubic-root) values and a cantellation construction I couldn't pin down to exact counts by hand; rather than ship wrong geometry, I left them out. I can add them if you can share
  the exact PolyhedronData vertex triples or want me to build them via a convex-hull cantellation.

